     "rate": "+0%",
     "text": "Zero lines of code was written by the person who built the first compiler. Most assume coding requires a math degree and years of memorizing syntax. Early programming was just flipping hardware switches and plugging wires into a giant plugboard. Then Grace Hopper developed the first compiler called A-Zero in nineteen fifty-two. She proved software could translate human logic into machine instructions automatically. But hardware was still painfully slow to program for everyday tasks. Therefore IBM created Fortran in nineteen fifty-seven as the first high level programming language. Programmers finally wrote readable formulas instead of punching raw numerical codes. Code evolved from physical copper wires into abstract human language. Software builds on layers of automation created decades ago. You stand on giants when you write your very first script."
    }
   }
